1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,192
A release manager reports: "The
same conflict keeps coming back."

2
00:00:08,232 --> 00:00:12,728
Every time main and the release branch
are merged, the judge temperature in the

3
00:00:12,768 --> 00:00:15,714
evaluation configuration
conflicts. They resolve it.

4
00:00:15,754 --> 00:00:20,365
Next week it conflicts again. And when
they open the base version of the file, to

5
00:00:20,405 --> 00:00:25,188
see what both sides started from, the base
version itself contains conflict markers.

6
00:00:25,228 --> 00:00:29,092
How can a starting point contain a
conflict? Take a guess, out loud.

7
00:00:33,932 --> 00:00:39,116
Nothing is corrupt. The two branches have
each merged the other, and they resolved

8
00:00:39,156 --> 00:00:44,149
the same conflict in opposite ways. The
history now has two equally good common

9
00:00:44,189 --> 00:00:49,373
ancestors, and Git has to reconcile those
first. To follow that, you need the rule

10
00:00:49,413 --> 00:00:54,533
by which every true merge is computed:
three inputs, one table, and nothing else.

11
00:00:54,573 --> 00:00:58,993
Keep those markers inside the base
in mind. You'll see them on screen.

12
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:04,464
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:01:04,504 --> 00:01:09,278
A commit is one saved snapshot of your
project, a branch is a name for a line of

14
00:01:09,318 --> 00:01:14,273
commits, and a merge combines two branches.
In the last video, a merge either moved

15
00:01:14,313 --> 00:01:19,328
a ref or reported that there was nothing to
do. Now both sides have commits that the

16
00:01:19,368 --> 00:01:22,397
other lacks, and Git has
to produce a new snapshot.

17
00:01:22,737 --> 00:01:27,060
The first half of this video is the rule
table: for one path, given the base

18
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:30,447
version, our version and their
version, what is the result?

19
00:01:30,487 --> 00:01:33,777
You'll check a real merge
against the table, path by path.

20
00:01:33,817 --> 00:01:38,599
The second half is the criss-cross history:
what happens when there is more than one

21
00:01:38,639 --> 00:01:41,814
merge base, and what the
default strategy does about it.

22
00:01:43,654 --> 00:01:45,233
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:45,573 --> 00:01:46,985
After this video you can:

24
00:01:47,325 --> 00:01:52,040
State the three-way rule for one path
as a table of base, ours and theirs.

25
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:56,243
Say which rows of the table are
decided without reading file content.

26
00:01:56,583 --> 00:02:00,818
Explain why a change made on both
sides and reverted on one comes back.

27
00:02:01,158 --> 00:02:05,698
Explain how two commits can have more
than one merge base, and what the default

28
00:02:05,738 --> 00:02:06,800
strategy does then.

29
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:13,719
The true merge. In one sentence: when both
sides have commits that the other lacks,

30
00:02:13,759 --> 00:02:18,715
Git combines the two tips against their
merge base, path by path and then line by

31
00:02:18,755 --> 00:02:22,293
line, and records the result
as a commit with two parents.

32
00:02:22,333 --> 00:02:27,350
The merge base, from the last video, is
the most recent commit that both histories

33
00:02:27,390 --> 00:02:27,844
contain.

34
00:02:28,184 --> 00:02:33,323
Three trees. Call them base, ours and
theirs. A tree here is the full snapshot of

35
00:02:33,363 --> 00:02:38,630
files that one commit records. Ours is the
commit HEAD points to, the branch you're

36
00:02:38,670 --> 00:02:41,892
on. Theirs is the commit
named on the command line.

37
00:02:41,932 --> 00:02:47,135
For every path, Git compares three blob
IDs. A blob is one file's content, and its

38
00:02:47,175 --> 00:02:49,245
ID is computed from that content.

39
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:53,568
The rule table. X, Y and Z
stand for different contents.

40
00:02:53,908 --> 00:02:57,045
Base X, ours X, theirs X: the
result is X. Nobody changed it.

41
00:02:57,385 --> 00:03:02,378
Base X, ours Y, theirs X: the
result is Y. Only ours changed it.

42
00:03:02,718 --> 00:03:07,589
Base X, ours X, theirs Y: the result
is Y. Only theirs changed it.

43
00:03:07,929 --> 00:03:13,088
Base X, ours Y, theirs Y: the result
is Y. Both made the same change.

44
00:03:13,428 --> 00:03:17,859
Base X, ours Y, theirs Z: merge
the content of the three files.

45
00:03:17,899 --> 00:03:21,052
This is the only row
that reads file content.

46
00:03:21,392 --> 00:03:28,523
Absent in base and ours, Y in
theirs: Y. Added by theirs.

47
00:03:28,863 --> 00:03:34,244
X in base and ours, absent in theirs: absent.
Deleted by theirs, untouched by ours.

48
00:03:34,584 --> 00:03:39,196
X in base, Y in ours, absent in theirs:
a conflict, called modify/delete.

49
00:03:39,536 --> 00:03:43,552
Absent in base, Y in ours, Z in
theirs: a conflict, called add/add.

50
00:03:43,892 --> 00:03:49,026
The table is not an interpretation. The
manual of git read-tree, Git's original

51
00:03:49,066 --> 00:03:54,463
three-way merge plumbing, states the first
rows in words such as "stage 2 and 3 are

52
00:03:54,503 --> 00:03:59,572
the same; take one or the other". Stages
1, 2 and 3 are base, ours and theirs.

53
00:03:59,912 --> 00:04:04,627
Two consequences. First, most paths
are settled without reading a file.

54
00:04:04,667 --> 00:04:09,783
Identical content has the identical blob
ID, as video 7 showed, and different

55
00:04:09,823 --> 00:04:13,935
content has a different one. So
comparing three IDs is enough.

56
00:04:14,275 --> 00:04:17,675
Second, the rows mention three
snapshots and nothing else.

57
00:04:17,715 --> 00:04:21,115
The commits between the base
and each tip don't take part.

58
00:04:21,155 --> 00:04:24,555
A change that both sides made
is taken once, and silently.

59
00:04:24,595 --> 00:04:29,419
And a change that one side made and later
undid counts as "that side did nothing".

60
00:04:29,759 --> 00:04:34,112
Quick quiz on that last sentence.
Both branches contain a change.

61
00:04:34,152 --> 00:04:38,235
One branch then reverts it, with
a new commit that undoes it.

62
00:04:38,275 --> 00:04:41,681
After the merge, is the
change A, gone, or B, back?

63
00:04:41,721 --> 00:04:42,492
Your answer?

64
00:04:47,332 --> 00:04:51,912
B. It comes back, and this is objective
three, so follow it to the end.

65
00:04:51,952 --> 00:04:57,313
At merge time, the tip of the branch that
reverted equals the base for those lines:

66
00:04:57,353 --> 00:05:02,714
row "X, X, Y" or "X, Y, X". The other side
changed it, and this side "did nothing".

67
00:05:02,754 --> 00:05:08,049
The result takes the change. The revert
has no vote, because the merge never looks

68
00:05:08,089 --> 00:05:12,539
at the commits in between. Almost
everyone is surprised by this once.

69
00:05:12,879 --> 00:05:17,889
Inside .git. A clean true merge writes a
blob for every file merged by content, the

70
00:05:17,929 --> 00:05:22,088
trees above those blobs, and one
commit object with two parent lines.

71
00:05:22,128 --> 00:05:27,077
Then it does what any commit does: the
current branch ref moves to the new commit,

72
00:05:27,117 --> 00:05:30,850
and the reflogs gain a line.
ORIG_HEAD holds the previous tip.

73
00:05:30,890 --> 00:05:32,249
git merge is 🟡 CAUTION.

74
00:05:32,589 --> 00:05:37,136
Criss-cross histories. In one sentence:
when two branches have each merged the

75
00:05:37,176 --> 00:05:42,018
other, they can have two best common
ancestors, and Git then merges those ancestors

76
00:05:42,058 --> 00:05:45,252
with each other first, and
uses the result as the base.

77
00:05:45,592 --> 00:05:49,082
Precisely. git merge-base
--all prints every merge base.

78
00:05:49,122 --> 00:05:54,314
Without --all you get one, and the manual
says "it is unspecified which best one is

79
00:05:54,354 --> 00:05:59,546
output". The default strategy, named ort,
handles the case as the manual describes:

80
00:05:59,586 --> 00:06:04,652
"When there is more than one common ancestor
that can be used for 3-way merge, it

81
00:06:04,692 --> 00:06:09,758
creates a merged tree of the common ancestors
and uses that as the reference tree

82
00:06:09,798 --> 00:06:14,297
for the 3-way merge". That merged tree
is called the virtual merge base.

83
00:06:14,637 --> 00:06:19,310
Inside .git: the virtual merge base is
computed during the merge and never becomes a

84
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:22,845
commit. Its only visible trace
is stage 1 of a conflicted path.

85
00:06:23,185 --> 00:06:28,260
Why Git does this. Either base alone would
silently reapply one side's old decision.

86
00:06:28,300 --> 00:06:32,157
A base that matches neither tip
returns the question to a human.

87
00:06:32,497 --> 00:06:37,486
When to avoid the situation. The textbook's
prevention: between long-lived branches,

88
00:06:37,526 --> 00:06:42,456
merge in one direction only. Keep other
branches short enough that they never merge

89
00:06:42,496 --> 00:06:43,114
each other.

90
00:06:44,954 --> 00:06:51,104
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
the editor-in-chief has three copies on the

91
00:06:51,144 --> 00:06:56,851
desk: Monday's, yours and theirs. If only
one of you changed a page, take that

92
00:06:56,891 --> 00:07:00,683
version. If both made the
same change, take it once.

93
00:07:00,723 --> 00:07:05,767
If both changed it differently, open
the page and compare paragraphs.

94
00:07:06,107 --> 00:07:10,997
The analogy breaks where it matters most:
the editor-in-chief reads for meaning.

95
00:07:11,037 --> 00:07:12,107
Git compares text.

96
00:07:12,447 --> 00:07:17,147
For the criss-cross: two Monday copies,
each containing a decision that one editor

97
00:07:17,187 --> 00:07:22,003
made and the other rejected. Before the
editor-in-chief can compare Friday's copies,

98
00:07:22,043 --> 00:07:25,067
the two Monday copies have
to be reconciled into one.

99
00:07:25,107 --> 00:07:29,750
The analogy breaks when they can't be: Git
then keeps the disagreement inside the

100
00:07:29,790 --> 00:07:31,253
base, as conflict markers.

101
00:07:31,593 --> 00:07:36,236
And the sentence to carry out of this
video: Git does not replay the branch.

102
00:07:36,276 --> 00:07:37,961
It looks at three endpoints.

103
00:07:39,801 --> 00:07:44,987
Now with real commits. This is
the merge from the last video.

104
00:07:45,027 --> 00:07:52,182
Base 23db174, ours 017bef5, theirs 67feed7,
and the result 6b10077 with two parents.

105
00:07:52,222 --> 00:07:58,693
Circle the three inputs. Everything the
merge knows is in those three trees.

106
00:07:59,033 --> 00:08:04,562
Two lines, and a cross between them:
each branch has merged the other.

107
00:08:04,602 --> 00:08:10,451
Follow the parents back from the two
tips. 5d28836 is reachable from both.

108
00:08:10,491 --> 00:08:14,509
So is 2725147. Neither is
an ancestor of the other.

109
00:08:14,549 --> 00:08:17,930
Two best common ancestors:
two merge bases.

110
00:08:28,970 --> 00:08:33,809
Into the lab. Three listings: the tree of
the base, of ours, and of theirs, with

111
00:08:33,849 --> 00:08:35,090
abbreviated blob IDs.

112
00:08:35,430 --> 00:08:39,412
Try it now, on paper. Stop the
recording here and do the work.

113
00:08:39,452 --> 00:08:43,759
For each path, compare the three
IDs and find its row in the table.

114
00:08:43,799 --> 00:08:49,013
Write down the result you expect: which
blob ID, or "absent", or "content merge".

115
00:08:49,053 --> 00:08:51,868
Seven paths. Then say
your answers out loud.

116
00:08:56,708 --> 00:09:02,998
git merge 🟡 CAUTION.

117
00:09:06,338 --> 00:09:10,117
One line says "Auto-merging":
one path needed a content merge.

118
00:09:10,457 --> 00:09:14,804
Read the result against the
table, and check your paper.

119
00:09:14,844 --> 00:09:21,308
README.md is af7f9b5 in base and theirs,
and aa477a1 in ours: only ours changed it,

120
00:09:21,348 --> 00:09:27,654
and the result is aa477a1. requirements.txt
changed only in theirs: the result is

121
00:09:27,694 --> 00:09:34,157
4a7b8a2. prompts/judge.txt is 38cb437 on
both sides, because Asha and you added the

122
00:09:34,197 --> 00:09:37,918
same line independently:
taken once, no message.

123
00:09:38,258 --> 00:09:44,343
scripts/legacy_eval.sh is unchanged in
ours and missing in theirs: deleted.

124
00:09:44,383 --> 00:09:50,959
evalkit/rouge.py exists only in theirs:
added. evalkit/metrics.py: nobody changed

125
00:09:50,999 --> 00:09:57,819
it. Only config/eval.yaml has three different
IDs, and its result, cef358f, is a new

126
00:09:57,859 --> 00:09:58,228
blob.

127
00:09:58,568 --> 00:10:03,559
Six of seven paths were decided by
comparing IDs. No file was read for them.

128
00:10:09,899 --> 00:10:14,839
The seventh. What ours changed since the
base, what theirs changed since the base,

129
00:10:14,879 --> 00:10:19,879
and the merged file with both changes. How
Git decides whether two such changes can

130
00:10:19,919 --> 00:10:21,944
both be applied is the next video.

131
00:10:22,284 --> 00:10:26,534
main and release/1.0 disagreed
about the judge temperature.

132
00:10:26,574 --> 00:10:30,461
Asha merged main into the
release branch and kept 0.7.

133
00:10:30,501 --> 00:10:35,551
At the same time you merged the
release branch into main and kept 0.0.

134
00:10:35,891 --> 00:10:45,296
git merge-base --all prints two commits:
2725147, her commit, and 5d28836, yours.

135
00:10:45,636 --> 00:10:50,772
What would happen if Git picked one? git
merge-tree 🟢 SAFE can be told which base to

136
00:10:50,812 --> 00:10:55,886
use. It writes objects and moves no ref.
Predict the temperature for each choice of

137
00:10:55,926 --> 00:10:57,242
base. Say it out loud.

138
00:11:02,082 --> 00:11:07,263
Two silent, opposite answers. Against
her commit as base, the release tip "did

139
00:11:07,303 --> 00:11:10,812
nothing" to the temperature,
and your side wins: 0.0.

140
00:11:10,852 --> 00:11:16,368
Against your commit as base, her side wins:
0.7. Neither would show a conflict, and

141
00:11:16,408 --> 00:11:21,924
nobody would have decided either. That's
the rule table doing exactly what it says,

142
00:11:21,964 --> 00:11:23,732
with the wrong Monday copy.

143
00:11:24,072 --> 00:11:25,540
The real merge does better.

144
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:30,748
A conflict. git ls-files -u lists three
entries for the path: stages 1, 2 and 3.

145
00:11:30,788 --> 00:11:35,840
And stage 1, the base version, contains
conflict markers, labelled "Temporary merge

146
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:39,705
branch 1" and "2". There's the
strange detail from the opening.

147
00:11:39,745 --> 00:11:44,858
That file is the virtual merge base: the
result of merging the two base commits with

148
00:11:44,898 --> 00:11:49,766
each other. Because both tips differ from
it, the outer merge conflicts, and the

149
00:11:49,806 --> 00:11:51,423
decision comes back to you.

150
00:11:51,763 --> 00:11:56,766
The script aborts the merge and repeats
it with the diff3 conflict style, which

151
00:11:56,806 --> 00:12:01,618
shows the nesting in one view. git merge
--abort is 🔴 DANGEROUS, so the five

152
00:12:01,658 --> 00:12:06,789
questions. What it changes: it resets the
index and the working tree to HEAD, and

153
00:12:06,829 --> 00:12:12,088
deletes the merge state. What it can destroy:
every resolution made so far, and any

154
00:12:12,128 --> 00:12:15,280
uncommitted edit that was
staged during the merge.

155
00:12:15,620 --> 00:12:19,208
How to preview: git status
and git diff --cached --stat.

156
00:12:19,248 --> 00:12:24,650
How to recover: staged content survives as
unreferenced objects that git fsck lists,

157
00:12:24,690 --> 00:12:30,092
and unstaged resolution edits are gone. When
it is appropriate: when the merge was a

158
00:12:30,132 --> 00:12:35,534
mistake or needs a different approach, as
here, where nothing has been resolved yet.

159
00:12:35,874 --> 00:12:40,730
Read the nested block: the inner markers
are nine characters long, the outer ones

160
00:12:40,770 --> 00:12:43,873
seven. The inner block is
the two earlier decisions.

161
00:12:43,913 --> 00:12:46,472
The outer sides are the
two current values.

162
00:12:48,312 --> 00:12:49,960
Five mistakes to watch for.

163
00:12:50,300 --> 00:12:55,290
Expecting Git to "replay" the branch's
commits. Root cause: a merge is computed from

164
00:12:55,330 --> 00:12:59,841
three snapshots; the commits between
the base and each tip do not take part.

165
00:13:00,181 --> 00:13:04,368
A reverted change comes back after a merge.
Root cause: the side that made and undid

166
00:13:04,408 --> 00:13:08,495
the change counts as having done nothing,
and the other side still has the change.

167
00:13:08,835 --> 00:13:14,129
Not noticing that both sides made the same
change. Root cause: identical changes are

168
00:13:14,169 --> 00:13:18,320
taken once and silently; the merge
prints no message for that row.

169
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:22,568
The base version of a conflicted
file contains conflict markers.

170
00:13:22,608 --> 00:13:27,380
Root cause: a criss-cross history with
two merge bases; ort merged them into a

171
00:13:27,420 --> 00:13:29,292
virtual base, markers included.

172
00:13:29,632 --> 00:13:33,441
Trusting git merge-base without
--all in a tangled history.

173
00:13:33,481 --> 00:13:38,790
Root cause: it prints one merge base, and
which best one is output is unspecified.

174
00:13:40,630 --> 00:13:45,155
Now, out of the lab. The usual producers
of a criss-cross, according to the

175
00:13:45,195 --> 00:13:50,269
textbook, are a release branch and main that
merge each other "to stay in sync", and

176
00:13:50,309 --> 00:13:53,678
two developers who each pull
the other's feature branch.

177
00:13:53,718 --> 00:13:57,452
The reported symptom is "the same
conflict keeps coming back".

178
00:13:57,492 --> 00:14:00,252
So run git merge-base
--all before theorizing.

179
00:14:00,592 --> 00:14:05,512
And a consequence of the true merge for
every team with CI, the automated tests that

180
00:14:05,552 --> 00:14:10,236
run on each change. The tree of a merge
commit is a snapshot that neither author

181
00:14:10,276 --> 00:14:14,960
wrote, and that no test has run on. Both
parents can be green while the merge is

182
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,441
red, so CI has to run on the merge result.

183
00:14:19,281 --> 00:14:25,880
Your turn. Do Exercise 6.2, Level 1,
"the merge base and the rule table", in

184
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:28,763
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

185
00:14:29,103 --> 00:14:32,475
Draw the three trees, base, ours and
theirs, before you run the merge.

186
00:14:32,515 --> 00:14:35,644
For each path, name the row of the
table and the expected result.

187
00:14:35,684 --> 00:14:37,009
Only then merge and compare.

188
00:14:38,849 --> 00:14:42,593
Q125: "What happens during
a three-way merge?"

189
00:14:44,933 --> 00:14:49,757
Answer out loud. The question is open on
purpose. A strong answer names the three

190
00:14:49,797 --> 00:14:54,742
inputs and how the third is found, gives
the per-path rule and says which rows need

191
00:14:54,782 --> 00:14:59,666
no file content, says what is written when
the merge succeeds, and states what the

192
00:14:59,706 --> 00:15:04,530
merge does not look at. If you have time
left, mention what happens when there is

193
00:15:04,570 --> 00:15:06,031
more than one merge base.

194
00:15:07,871 --> 00:15:13,230
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say: a true merge combines three trees:

195
00:15:13,270 --> 00:15:18,430
the merge base, ours and theirs. For each
path, unchanged on one side takes the

196
00:15:18,470 --> 00:15:22,051
other side. Changed identically
takes that change once.

197
00:15:22,091 --> 00:15:27,054
Changed differently needs a content
merge. And modify/delete or add/add is a

198
00:15:27,094 --> 00:15:27,647
conflict.

199
00:15:27,987 --> 00:15:33,018
Most paths are decided by comparing blob
IDs. The merge does not look at the commits

200
00:15:33,058 --> 00:15:37,305
in between, so a change reverted on
one side comes back from the other.

201
00:15:37,345 --> 00:15:41,591
When two branches have merged each
other, there can be two merge bases.

202
00:15:41,631 --> 00:15:46,602
ort merges them into a virtual base, and
if that inner merge conflicts, the markers

203
00:15:46,642 --> 00:15:47,689
appear in stage 1.

204
00:15:49,529 --> 00:15:53,193
Read sections 8.4 and 8.5 of Chapter 8.

205
00:15:53,533 --> 00:15:58,138
Draw the three trees for Exercise 6.2 before
you run it, if you have not done so in

206
00:15:58,178 --> 00:15:59,370
the practice exercise.

